WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:11.080
MCP systems move data through prompts, user files, retrieved resources, tool results, server metadata, logs, cached content, memory features, and downstream services.

2
00:00:11.080 --> 00:00:13.219
Each movement changes exposure.

3
00:00:13.219 --> 00:00:23.306
A file that was safe in a private workspace may become riskier if placed into a model context, copied into logs, cached for later use, or sent to a remote server.

4
00:00:23.306 --> 00:00:30.488
Data handling rules should describe what can enter the system, where it can be stored, and how long it can remain.

5
00:00:30.488 --> 00:00:36.219
Trust boundaries are points where data, identity, permissions, or assumptions change.

6
00:00:36.219 --> 00:00:40.422
User input crosses a boundary when it influences model behavior.

7
00:00:40.422 --> 00:00:44.319
External content crosses a boundary when it enters context.

8
00:00:44.319 --> 00:00:49.820
Tool results cross a boundary when they move from a downstream system back to the host.

9
00:00:49.820 --> 00:00:54.787
Server metadata crosses a boundary when it influences model tool selection.

10
00:00:54.787 --> 00:01:01.664
Data flow diagrams should show these transitions instead of treating the AI application as one opaque box.

11
00:01:01.664 --> 00:01:05.255
External content should be treated as untrusted input.

12
00:01:05.255 --> 00:01:12.820
A document, web page, issue, email, repository file, or resource may contain inaccurate, stale, or misleading text.

13
00:01:12.820 --> 00:01:18.857
That content can still be useful, but it should not override trusted configuration or policy.

14
00:01:18.857 --> 00:01:23.594
Provenance helps the system and user understand where content came from.

15
00:01:23.594 --> 00:01:30.318
Tenant isolation and per-user filtering prevent one user's data from becoming another user's context.

16
00:01:30.318 --> 00:01:34.903
Data minimization reduces how much sensitive information is exposed.

17
00:01:34.903 --> 00:01:37.960
Retention and logging require careful design.

18
00:01:37.960 --> 00:01:46.671
Logs can support investigation, but they should avoid unnecessary secrets, credentials, regulated data, or sensitive user content.

19
00:01:46.671 --> 00:01:52.783
Cached content and memory can improve usability, but they need boundaries and deletion rules.

20
00:01:52.783 --> 00:01:57.903
Sensitive data should be protected before it reaches the model where possible.

21
00:01:57.903 --> 00:02:07.684
MCP fundamentals are not only about connecting systems; they are about keeping information understandable and controlled as it moves across systems.
